    "meta": {
      "_et_gb_content_width": "",
      "_et_pb_old_content": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>I've had change on my mind, and it's no wonder why.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>As vaccines are distributed, perhaps you, like me, have found yourself with more questions than you would have expected.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>What will the world look like in the coming weeks and months? Who will we be as people? How will we interact with the&nbsp;world alongside which we changed?</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The conclusion I've reached? We'll be the same <strong>as before, but different. </strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>Lessons from Writing</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The more I considered this notion of <em>as before, but different</em>, the less surprising it became. <strong>Change underwrites all storytelling</strong>, after all, and for me it's been <strong>the foundation of arriving at a better understanding of myself.</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>For example, someone in my circles recently mentioned one either writes to music or one does not; there is no middle ground. On its face, I understood the motivation for this statement. It's oft-repeated, and whether you're a writer or not, you likely know whether you embrace music as the backdrop to your toilings or if silence is more your speed (or sound, as it were).</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>But then, I remembered.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Years ago,</strong> particularly when I first started putting pen to paper for my earliest works of fiction, <strong>I required absolute silence to get words on the page.</strong> Over time, however—and I'm not sure when, exactly—the need to drown out other noises (mewling cats, most likely) drove me to start playing music, softly, in the background.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Now? I can't write without music of some kind directing my focus.</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The same has been the case for me where writing on a schedule is concerned, what with my having spent years writing whenever I had a free minute before finally instituting a regular routine. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Then there's the Multiple Manuscript Mystery. How does one go about actively working on more than one manuscript at a time? For so long, I never had an answer to that question. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>I could, of course, draft one manuscript in the morning while querying another in the afternoon, but <strong>actively revising two manuscripts across back-to-back hours? Unthinkable!</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Or, well, it was unthinkable until I gave it a try.</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>As Before, But Different</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>When first undergoing these mindset shifts, I struggled. In no case was it as if a switch flipped overnight, and the process is, in so many ways, ongoing. Even for these now long-entrenched habits of writing while music plays or doing my daily writing duty at dawn, I must sometimes make exceptions.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>But in the end, it all integrates. The writing goes on, but at a different time of day, to a different soundtrack, and with any number of manuscripts in any number of stages. <strong>What changes, really, is not the routine so much as my outlook, and it's for this shift that I'm most grateful.</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>No matter the scale, these changes tend toward a new balance</strong>, and though one might be discomfited to know balance is ever-fleeting—that future is never truly certain—I take solace in this—</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>What comes next is always, in some way, that which was before.</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>Going Forward</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>So, what <em>will </em>the world look like in the coming weeks and months? Who will we be as people? How will we interact with the world alongside which we've changed?</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>No one knows the answers to those questions—not precisely—but what we do know is <strong>the world will be as before, but different, and I'm very much looking forward to the latter.</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
