    "comment_status": "open",
    "content": {
      "protected": false,
      "rendered": "<p><span class="embed-youtube" style="text-align:center; display: block;"><iframe loading="lazy" class="youtube-player" width="1080" height="608" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/1oNfJbhfV5Y?version=3&#038;rel=1&#038;showsearch=0&#038;showinfo=1&#038;iv_load_policy=1&#038;fs=1&#038;hl=en-US&#038;autohide=2&#038;wmode=transparent" allowfullscreen="true" style="border:0;" sandbox="allow-scripts allow-same-origin allow-popups allow-presentation allow-popups-to-escape-sandbox"></iframe></span></p>
<p>When most writers think of needing help, it comes in the form of critique partners or beta readers, but there are so many other times we as writers might need a helping hand. In today's meditation, exploration, and affirmation, we explore the when, how, and why of asking for assistance. Why? Because it's dangerous to go alone.</p>
<span class="et_bloom_bottom_trigger"></span>",
    },
    "date": "2020-07-02T08:15:17",
